A||||5||flexableface||flomasters||||5||
Z||000000||flexableface||10-02-2000||09:15 AM||||i was wondering on what kind of flomaster muffler i should use to replace the stock muffler. I would like it to have kind of a louder and deeper sound than stock. i also plan on taking out the catalitic conveter. can the flomaster be welded in place of where the stock one was? also is it possible that i can do it me self. thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||10-02-2000||10:06 AM||||you can do it yourself but its a BIG pain in the butt... i had a Flowmaster 40 series (not a delta flow) on my 86 SE and she sounds SOOO MEAN  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> GT Bastard has it and seems happy with it... you might want to also check <A HREF="http://www.goravin.com" TARGET=_blank>www.goravin.com</A>  or <A HREF="http://www.brullenexhaust.com" TARGET=_blank>www.brullenexhaust.com</A>  for other  ideas.||||reg||1
Z||000002||flexableface||10-02-2000||02:40 PM||||also which is louder the 40 or 80 series. i think i also am going to put it on myself. Because i read that one of the member had i put on for 200 dollars and at my local custom exhuast shop the will put a custom exhuast on for 250 including stainless steel tips. i wanted to spend as little as posible and have a good sounding exhuaust.||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||10-02-2000||04:40 PM||||40 series is louder... and lighter too! but it's not loud enough to be annoying in the car.. since there is no muffler under any part of the passanger compartment you can't really hear it in the car... but you sure can outside!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||rockcrawl||10-02-2000||07:45 PM||||According to Flowmaster, the 40 series and 80 series have almost exactly the same noise levels, both inside and ouside the car. The 40 series delta flow is considerably quieter than the others. The 40 series requires some pretty extensive mods if you want dual outlets, there is no dual out crossflow 40 series. Chris west sells modified delta flow 40's <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com/Exhaust/Muffler.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cwestco.com/Exhaust/Muffler.html</A>  . I've used the 80 series muffler on a 3800 conversion, and it was quite a challenge to fit the huge thing in there. It sounded great though. I'd recommend leaving the converter or installing a high flow converter, otherwise it gets too loud IMO.<BR>Whichever one you choose, it's going to take some work, not just a "weld in place" replacement.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000005||AL||10-03-2000||10:54 AM||||well talkin from experience,i installed the trans am flowmaster i took the old one out and cut the old muffler and then took my pieces with me to the shop and they welded it in place for 70 bucks,the sound is wicked so much i,ve had people come to me and say that is the healthiest fiero i,ve heard,so take it off your self and get your flowy on, you won,t regret it.<B>AL</B> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AL_sig.gif">                              <P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||14
